# Disrupting Labor in Digital Humanities; or, The Classroom Is Not Your Crowd

**Authors:** Spencer D.C. Keralis

## Abstract
<jats:p>Digital  humanists  have  a  labor  problem,  but  it’s  not  what  you  might  think.  It’s  not  about  humanities  faculty  getting  credit  in  tenure and promotion for digital informatics work. It’s not about the adjunctification of teaching labor. It’s not about the devalua-tion of humanities PhDs, the contraction of the faculty job mar-ket,  the  rise  of  #alt-ac,  nor  the  para-professionalization  of  aca-demic libraries. And while I care deeply about these problems, at least three of which affect me personally almost every day, what I  am  concerned  with  here  is  a  type  of  labor  that  the  collective  preoccupation with these issues effaces: the use of student labor on digital humanities projects in and out of the classroom.</jats:p>

**Source:** https://doi.org/10.21983/p3.0230.1.18

